@node Diagnostics
@chapter Diagnostics

libdtfl carries a port of the timely diagnostics tools: a live console
that reconstructs the operator graph, scheduling and arrangement sizes
of a running computation.  It is built on the structured event logging
every worker produces.

@section Structured event logging

A worker emits structured events on named streams: @code{"timely"} (the
dataflow structure and scheduling), @code{"differential/arrange"}
(arrangement batch, merge and trace events) and @code{"communication"}
(message send and receive, thread state).  This is distinct from the
human-readable diagnostic sink of @code{dtfl_set_log_hook}
(@pxref{Error handling}); the structured log carries machine-readable
events.

A program subscribes to a stream and receives each batch of events
already decoded, so a C caller never touches the wire bytes.  Register
before the workers start with @code{dtfl_config_log_subscribe}, so every
worker installs the subscriber as it comes up, or on a single worker you
own with @code{dtfl_worker_log_subscribe}.  The subscriber is called on
the worker thread with a @code{dtfl_log_batch}; the accessors
(@code{dtfl_log_batch_len}, @code{dtfl_log_event_kind_at} and the
per-event field readers) read it.  The batch and every pointer it hands
out are valid only for the duration of the call.

@section The in-process console

The diagnostics session subscribes to a worker's @code{"timely"} and
@code{"differential/arrange"} streams, keeps the live set of operators,
channels and statistics, and serves them to browser clients over a
WebSocket as a stream of JSON frames.  The upstream console renders the
frames unmodified.

@example
dtfl_diag_session *s = dtfl_diag_session_new(worker);
dtfl_diag_server *srv = NULL;
if (dtfl_worker_index(worker) == 0)
        dtfl_diag_server_start(s, "127.0.0.1", 51371, &srv);
/* build and run the user dataflow; step the worker as usual */
dtfl_diag_server_stop(srv);
dtfl_diag_session_free(s);
@end example

The session is worker-owned: create it, pump it and free it on the
worker thread.  The server runs its accept and send loop on a background
thread and talks to the session through an internal queue, so no session
handle crosses the thread boundary.

By default the server binds @code{127.0.0.1}, so operator names and
timings are not published to the network without authentication; this is
a deliberate divergence from the upstream default of @code{0.0.0.0}
(@pxref{Porting notes}).  Pass a different interface to
@code{dtfl_diag_server_start} to restore the upstream behavior.

@section The command-line tool

@command{dtfl-diag} is the out-of-process counterpart, the C port of
timely's @command{tdiag}.  It reconstructs the operator graph
(@command{graph}), the timing profile (@command{profile}) or the
arrangement sizes (@command{arrangements}) of a computation, from log
streams the source program ships to it over TCP, or from a recorded
stream read with @option{-f}.  A source program connects its loggers to
the address @command{dtfl-diag} listens on through
@code{dtfl_config_from_env}, which reads @env{TIMELY_WORKER_LOG_ADDR}
and @env{DIFFERENTIAL_LOG_ADDR}.  See @code{dtfl-diag(1)} for the full
set of options.
